From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 19 Aug 2026 17:15:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 42/42] vc4gfx: do not free the framebuffer before knowing one
 can be had

The Pi 3 boots black with this driver, and the two observations that
matter came from the same pair of runs: with `mungwall` the boot dies
before any bitmap is created and the splash stays on the screen with the
clock running, and without it the driver initialises and the screen goes
black. The display was alive right up to the moment vc4gfx touched it.

`vc4_program_fb()` says why. It opens with VCTAG_FBFREE -- "free any
previous framebuffer the firmware was holding" -- and only then asks for a
new one. The firmware drops the surface *and* the display list that
scanned it out, so a FBALLOC that comes back empty-handed leaves nothing
to put back. That is exactly the state the dump described: HDMI channel
enabled, running, correct 1080p timings, and a display list consisting of
one END word.

FBALLOC reallocates over an existing framebuffer by itself. The FBFREE
bought nothing and cost the one thing this code cannot recover.

Second half of the same failure: a tagged reply carrying a null pointer
is the firmware saying it could not allocate, and this read it as
success. fb_ptr came back NULL, the caller carried on, and on this port
patches/aros/0037 then quietly substituted the kernel's boot surface --
so the log showed a healthy-looking framebuffer address that the firmware
was no longer scanning out, and the HVS got blamed for showing nothing.
A null pointer or a zero size now retries with fewer pages and, failing
that, fails, saying what it asked for and what it got.

Neither half is confirmed on hardware yet. What is confirmed is the
sequence: display alive before the driver, gone after, and the only thing
in between that can free a framebuffer is the call that frees it.
--- a/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_onbitmap.c
+++ b/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_onbitmap.c
@@ -161,14 +161,21 @@
          * (FBFREE -> batched mode/depth/pixfmt/FBALLOC -> GETPITCH). */
         VC4_MBOX_LOCK(xsd);
 
-        /* Free any previous framebuffer the firmware was holding. */
-        xsd->vcsd_MBoxMessage[0] = AROS_LE2LONG(6 * 4);
-        xsd->vcsd_MBoxMessage[1] = AROS_LE2LONG(VCTAG_REQ);
-        xsd->vcsd_MBoxMessage[2] = AROS_LE2LONG(VCTAG_FBFREE);
-        xsd->vcsd_MBoxMessage[3] = 0;
-        xsd->vcsd_MBoxMessage[4] = 0;
-        xsd->vcsd_MBoxMessage[5] = 0;
-        MBoxCall((void *)VCMB_BASE, VCMB_PROPCHAN, xsd->vcsd_MBoxMessage);
+        /*
+         * No FBFREE first.
+         *
+         * Releasing the firmware's framebuffer before knowing a replacement
+         * can be had is what turns a failed allocation into a machine with no
+         * display at all: the firmware drops the surface *and* the display
+         * list that scanned it out, and if FBALLOC then comes back
+         * empty-handed there is nothing left to put back. On a Pi 3 that is
+         * exactly what happened -- black screen, HDMI channel enabled and
+         * running, and its display list a single END word.
+         *
+         * FBALLOC reallocates over an existing framebuffer on its own, so the
+         * FBFREE bought nothing and cost the one thing that cannot be
+         * recovered from here.
+         */
 
         /* SETRES + SETVRES + SETDEPTH + SETPIXFMT + FBALLOC in one batch.
          * SETPIXFMT pins channel order to RGB to match pftags_32bpp (R in
@@ -219,6 +226,25 @@
         fb_ptr = (APTR)(IPTR)(AROS_LE2LONG(xsd->vcsd_MBoxMessage[23]) & 0x3fffffff);
         fb_size = AROS_LE2LONG(xsd->vcsd_MBoxMessage[24]);
 
+        /*
+         * A tagged reply with a null pointer is the firmware saying it could
+         * not allocate -- not enough left in the VideoCore split for the mode
+         * asked for. That is a failure, and it used to read as success: the
+         * caller carried on with a NULL framebuffer, which on this port then
+         * quietly fell back to the kernel's boot surface and blamed the HVS
+         * for showing nothing.
+         */
+        if (!fb_ptr || !fb_size)
+        {
+            bug("[VideoCoreGfx] FBALLOC refused %ux%u %ubpp, %d page(s): "
+                "ptr=0x%p size=%u\n", aligned_width, height * pages,
+                (unsigned)bitsperpixel, pages, fb_ptr, (unsigned)fb_size);
+            VC4_MBOX_UNLOCK(xsd);
+            if (pages > 1)
+                continue;
+            return FALSE;
+        }
+
         /* Use the firmware-reported pitch verbatim: it may align rows, or
          * (QEMU) keep a default virtual width that ignores our request.
          */
